1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,199
A price tells you what the crowd believes.

2
00:00:02,199 --> 00:00:04,450
It doesn't tell you who could already know.

3
00:00:04,650 --> 00:00:08,803
LevelField is a pre-trade risk layer for DreamDEX event contracts.

4
00:00:08,803 --> 00:00:11,382
Before a trader or an agent takes a side,

5
00:00:11,382 --> 00:00:15,346
it measures the field itself: structural information asymmetry.

6
00:00:15,546 --> 00:00:19,825
Start with a real DreamDEX price binary, captured on Somnia Shannon.

7
00:00:19,825 --> 00:00:23,349
LevelField scores it three out of one hundred. Low risk.

8
00:00:23,549 --> 00:00:26,159
Bitcoin's closing price answers to no one.

9
00:00:26,159 --> 00:00:28,024
The outcome is public, global,

10
00:00:28,024 --> 00:00:31,318
and disclosed to everyone at almost the same instant.

11
00:00:31,518 --> 00:00:33,442
Now change nothing but the event.

12
00:00:33,442 --> 00:00:37,698
This curated reference contract settles on one person's private decision.

13
00:00:37,898 --> 00:00:39,604
Same engine, same rules.

14
00:00:39,604 --> 00:00:42,590
Ninety-five out of one hundred. High risk.

15
00:00:42,790 --> 00:00:44,577
One person controls the outcome,

16
00:00:44,577 --> 00:00:47,871
and no clear rule stops an early knower from trading on it.

17
00:00:47,871 --> 00:00:50,606
A circuit breaker floors this score at high risk.

18
00:00:50,806 --> 00:00:53,817
That gap, three to ninety-five, is the product.

19
00:00:53,817 --> 00:00:58,109
LevelField explains who can know first — never which side will win.

20
00:00:58,309 --> 00:01:01,049
No accusations. No live surveillance.

21
00:01:01,049 --> 00:01:03,270
Just structure, read honestly.

22
00:01:03,470 --> 00:01:07,058
Underneath sits a public anchor library: five dimensions,

23
00:01:07,058 --> 00:01:10,206
from outcome control to outcome manufacturability.

24
00:01:10,206 --> 00:01:13,354
A model classifies each one against those anchors.

25
00:01:13,554 --> 00:01:16,837
Every evidence quote must match the contract word for word.

26
00:01:16,837 --> 00:01:21,011
Text that tries to talk to the assessor is caught by code and disqualified.

27
00:01:21,211 --> 00:01:25,065
And when information is missing, the engine refuses to guess low.

28
00:01:25,065 --> 00:01:26,903
Unknown defaults to level four.

29
00:01:27,103 --> 00:01:29,402
Then deterministic code takes over —

30
00:01:29,402 --> 00:01:32,914
fixed weights, cross-dimension rules, circuit breakers.

31
00:01:32,914 --> 00:01:35,788
The model never writes the number. Code does.

32
00:01:35,988 --> 00:01:41,452
Here, an agent calls the real LevelField MCP server over standard input and output,

33
00:01:41,452 --> 00:01:42,440
before it acts.

34
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:46,079
The policy fits in one line: low and moderate proceed.

35
00:01:46,079 --> 00:01:47,735
Elevated and high decline.

36
00:01:47,935 --> 00:01:50,627
The DreamDEX binary returns PROCEED at three.

37
00:01:50,627 --> 00:01:54,216
The individual-decision case returns DECLINE at ninety-five,

38
00:01:54,216 --> 00:01:55,711
with the reason attached.

39
00:01:55,911 --> 00:02:01,526
After a provenance-complete republish, every current score will live on Somnia Shannon —

40
00:02:01,526 --> 00:02:04,397
its band, five dimension levels, method hash,

41
00:02:04,397 --> 00:02:07,076
timestamp, and an immutable source record.

42
00:02:07,276 --> 00:02:09,362
A verifier reads every field back,

43
00:02:09,362 --> 00:02:12,307
and fails closed on anything missing or changed.

44
00:02:12,507 --> 00:02:20,318
Across sixteen curated contracts, scores span three to ninety-five, and category risk rises in exactly the expected order —

45
00:02:20,318 --> 00:02:22,541
a Spearman rho of point nine three.

46
00:02:22,741 --> 00:02:26,806
Sixty-nine software tests and eight smart-contract tests pass.

47
00:02:26,806 --> 00:02:31,921
The official DreamDEX SDK independently cross-checks active-market discovery —

48
00:02:31,921 --> 00:02:34,215
read-only, no private key required.

49
00:02:34,415 --> 00:02:39,256
Building this surfaced eleven evidence-backed SDK and documentation findings.

50
00:02:39,256 --> 00:02:44,285
As event contracts grow beyond price, one question decides who keeps their edge.

51
00:02:44,285 --> 00:02:46,423
Know who can know — before you do.

